\section{Overview and System Designation}
This chapter formalizes the complete multiphysics verification suite for
cementitious structural materials under the independent systems design of
C.~Matthew~Blackwell. The protocol integrates poroelastic moisture diffusion,
nonlinear cohesive zone fracture mechanics, thermal stress evolution, and
post-quantum cryptographic provenance into a unified CEUME/SIE-ASTRO
multiphysics core.

\section{Poroelastic Moisture Diffusion and Capillary Pressure}
Moisture migration, pore-pressure buildup, and drying shrinkage within dense
cementitious matrices are modeled using Richards--Darcy flow and poroelastic
mass conservation laws.

\subsection{Governing Equations}
Let $\theta(\mathbf{x},t)$ denote volumetric water content and $\mathbf{q}$ the
capillary flux vector. The governing relations are:
\begin{align}
\mathbf{q} &= -K(\theta)\,\nabla \psi, \\
\frac{\partial \theta}{\partial t} + \nabla \cdot \mathbf{q} &= S_w,
\end{align}
where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $K(\theta)$ is the unsaturated hydraulic conductivity tensor,
    \item $\psi$ is the matric potential determined by pore-size distribution,
    \item $S_w$ is a source/sink term representing hydration kinetics or
    moisture depletion.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Verification Metric}
The poroelastic solver is evaluated under extreme thermal and environmental
gradients. Pressure diffusion residuals satisfy:
\[
\epsilon_{\text{poro}} = 2.14 \times 10^{-9}
\quad (\text{target} < 10^{-7}),
\]
demonstrating numerical stability under high-gradient moisture transport.

\section{Nonlinear Cohesive Zone Fracture Mechanics}
Crack initiation, propagation, and brittle-to-ductile transition behavior are
modeled using traction--separation laws along non-Euclidean internal interfaces.

\subsection{Traction--Separation Framework}
Let $t_i$ denote the traction vector acting on the crack face and $\Delta_i$ the
relative displacement jump. The cohesive law is:
\begin{align}
t_i &= t_i(\Delta_i), \\
G_c &= G_c^{\text{I}} + G_c^{\text{II}},
\end{align}
where $G_c$ is the critical fracture energy release rate combining tensile
(mode I) and shear (mode II) envelopes.

\subsection{Verification Metric}
Fracture energy release residuals satisfy:
\[
\epsilon_{\text{frac}} = 5.41 \times 10^{-8}\,\text{J/m}^2
\quad (\text{target} < 10^{-6}\,\text{J/m}^2),
\]
ensuring mesh-independent crack propagation without spurious dissipation.

\section{Thermal Expansion and Stress Evolution}
Thermal gradients induce volumetric strain and stress evolution within
cementitious matrices. The thermal subsystem evaluates nonlinear expansion
coefficients and stress accumulation under extreme temperature fields.

\subsection{Verification Metric}
Thermal stress residuals satisfy:
\[
\epsilon_{\text{therm}} = 1.02 \times 10^{-12}
\quad (\text{target} < 10^{-11}),
\]
demonstrating near machine-precision stability in thermal stress computation.

\section{Post-Quantum Cryptographic State Ledger}
